stions about the relation between literary forms and the socio-historical context within which they take on their meanings, and about the historical formation of Asian American identities. This course satisfies the Literatures of the Americas requirement for English majors.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, LA-AS, SCD-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(EC-SAP)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2839, "crseId": 371642, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "AAS", "catalogNbr": "3378", "titleShort": "Korean American Literature", "titleLong": "Korean American Literature", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 17617, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:25AM", "timeEnd": "12:40PM", "startDt": "01/23/2023", "endDt": "05/09/2023", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "isy4", "firstName": "Ivanna", "middleName": "Sang Een", "lastName": "Yi"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Rockefeller Hall 104", "bldgDescr": "Rockefeller Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "RCK104", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/23/2023", "endDt": "05/20/2023", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ASIAN", "catalogNbr": "3378", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "COML", "catalogNbr": "3378", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/23/2023", "sessionEndDt": "05/20/2023", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "The rapidly growing\u00a0literature\u00a0of the\u00a0Korean\u00a0diaspora is one of the most significant developments in Korean literature since the 20th century. As Korean literature has circulated as world literature, it has become more widely recognized in the Anglophone world through translation and through narratives written by Korean American authors. This course will explore Korean American literature and creative transpacific exchanges between Korea and the US, addressing issues of identity, language, place, migration, race discrimination, citizenship, and the ways in which storytelling shapes community. We will examine the vibrant dialogue between works of fiction and poetry across the Pacific, reading the work of Korean American authors alongside the writing of Korean authors working in the Korean language. Increasingly,\u00a0Korean\u00a0American\u00a0writers are creating narratives that remember and reconfigure\u00a0Korean\u00a0history and Korea's relationship to the US, and we will explore narratives and poetry that offer new perspectives on the Japanese colonial period, the\u00a0Korean\u00a0War, and American imperialism such as Min Jin Lee's Pachinko, Chang-rae Lee's\u00a0The Surrendered, and Don Mee Choi's\u00a0DMZ Colony. Readings for the course will be in English or in English translation and no prior knowledge of Korea is required.", "catalogBreadth": "(GB)", "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, LA-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null,